#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Sleep idle Claude Code agents running in Herdr panes; wake them on demand.

Why this exists (measured 2026-09-26): 15 `claude` processes summed to 2.0 GB
RSS on a 16 GB machine that was already 6.5 GB into an 8 GB swap file, and
14 of the 15 lived in Herdr panes that had been idle for 5-6 hours. Orca has
"Agent hibernation" for this (Settings → Experimental), but it only covers
Orca-managed terminals. Herdr has no equivalent, and per its CONTRIBUTING.md
it does not accept unsolicited PRs — so the policy lives here, outside both
tools, driven entirely through Herdr's public CLI.

What "sleep" means here, and why it is safe:

1. **The pane stays; only the agent exits.** We submit `/exit` to Claude,
   which returns the pane to its shell prompt. We never `herdr pane close`,
   because `herdr agent start` requires "an existing pane at an interactive
   shell prompt" — the pane *is* the restart slot, and closing it would also
   throw away the user's layout.
2. **The resume handle is Claude's own session UUID**, which Herdr already
   tracks per pane (`agent_session.value`, reported by the official Claude
   integration's hooks). Wake is `herdr agent start <name> --kind claude
   --pane <id> -- <original argv> --resume <uuid>`. Verified 2026-09-26:
   exit → agent gone in 1 s, PID dead; wake → identical UUID, prior context
   answered correctly.
3. **Idle time comes from the transcript, not from Herdr.** Herdr exposes no
   timestamps (only a `state_change_seq` counter), so instead of a daemon
   keeping its own clock we read the mtime of
   `~/.claude/projects/*/<uuid>.jsonl`, which Claude rewrites on every turn.
   That survives Herdr and machine restarts and needs no state. When more
   than one file matches the UUID (a moved project), the newest wins, so a
   stale copy can never make a busy session look idle.
4. **Everything fails closed.** The rules come from Orca's hibernation
   implementation and its bug history (stablyai/orca#22657, #16279, #15625,
   #18731) and from a four-model review of this script (2026-09-26): status
   must be `idle` or `done`; the pane must not be focused; the agent must be
   `claude` with a session UUID and an on-disk transcript; the UUID must not
   be open in any other pane; the transcript must be older than the idle
   window; the composer must be *positively* empty (a draft, an unreadable
   screen, or an unrecognised prompt all refuse — `/exit` would destroy a
   draft); the original argv must be safely replayable (`--fork-session`, a
   positional prompt, `--print`, `--session-id` and anything after `--`
   refuse — replaying them would fork the session or re-run work); and all
   of it is re-checked immediately before `/exit`, since a scan tick is
   minutes old by then. `--exclude` protects orchestrators that
   legitimately sit idle waiting for mail. Every scan or wake runs under a
   file lock, and the journal is re-read under that lock before each write.
   A malformed config aborts `scan`/`install` rather than silently running
   with no excludes; `wake`/`list`/`log` still work.
5. **A wake handle is never thrown away on uncertainty.** If Claude has not
   left the pane after the exit wait, the entry stays with
   `phase: exit-requested` and the next scan reconciles it (still running →
   dropped as a no-op; gone → `asleep`). If the pane vanished, the entry is
   kept and the manual resume command printed. Wake refuses if the UUID is
   live in any pane or `claude` process, or if that cannot be determined.
6. **The pane advertises how to wake itself.** After exit we reset terminal
   modes (a dead TUI can leave the shell receiving mouse packets), print a
   `💤 … wake: herdr-sleeper wake <name>` line into the pane and prefix the
   pane label with 💤.

Trade-off to know: `claude --resume` replays the whole transcript, so every
wake costs a full prompt-cache write. Pick the window accordingly — the
operator here uses 12 h; the default is 12 h; anything from minutes to days
is valid.

Configuration, lowest to highest precedence:
  defaults → ~/.config/herdr-sleeper/config.toml → HERDR_SLEEPER_* env →
  CLI flags. `herdr-sleeper config` prints the effective values and where
  each came from. Example config.toml:

    idle = "12h"                    # or idle_hours = 12; "90m", "1d" also work
    exclude = ["orc", "orc-meta"]   # agent names or pane ids never slept
    interval_minutes = 30           # used by `install`

State (override with HERDR_SLEEPER_STATE): ~/.local/state/herdr-sleeper/
  sleeping.json  journal of slept panes (the wake handles), with a phase
  panes.json     uuid→argv snapshot of every agent pane, merged each scan,
                 so a crash still leaves a wake handle (`wake` falls back to it)
  events.jsonl   one JSON object per sleep / wake / refusal — `herdr-sleeper log`
  sleeper.log    the same, human-readable
  lock           the interprocess lock

Usage:
  herdr-sleeper scan  [--idle 12h | --idle-hours H] [--dry-run] [--only PANE ...]
                      [--exclude NAME_OR_PANE ...] [--json]
  herdr-sleeper wake  (<name>|<pane_id>|--all)
  herdr-sleeper list  [--json]
  herdr-sleeper log   [-n N] [--json]
  herdr-sleeper config [--json]
  herdr-sleeper install [--interval-minutes M] | uninstall   (launchd; prints a cron line elsewhere)
"""

